# HopOn Netherlands

HopOn is an Angular 22 and Spring Boot 4 local-first guide to Amsterdam, Rotterdam and Utrecht. Explore loads all three cities together in one shared catalog and map; search and category filters apply across the catalog, and every place card identifies its city. Discover by Vibe is deterministic, not AI, and remains available in supported narrower layouts (up to 1100px). Day plans can combine places from all three cities. The app does not provide transit routing, user accounts or a hosted production service.

## Requirements

- Windows PowerShell; Docker is not needed.
- Java 27. `.tools\run-backend.ps1` uses a verified `JAVA_HOME` or `.tools\jdk-27` and can bootstrap a local JDK when neither exists.
- The committed Gradle wrapper uses Gradle 9.8.2.
- Node.js `^22.22.3 || ^24.15.0 || ^26.0.0` and npm 11.19.0, as declared in `frontend/package.json`.

## Start the app

Terminal 1, from the repository root:

```powershell
.\.tools\run-backend.ps1
```

This starts Spring on `127.0.0.1:8080`.

Terminal 2, from the repository root:

```powershell
Set-Location .\frontend
npm.cmd ci
npm.cmd start
```

Open <http://localhost:4200>. Angular proxies relative `/api/**` requests to Spring. The Playwright server uses port 4300.

For a phone on the same trusted Wi-Fi, run `npm.cmd run start:lan` in `frontend/` and open `http://<PC-LAN-IP>:4200`. Allow the Angular dev server on Windows Private networks only if prompted. Keep backend port 8080 on loopback; do not expose it to the LAN or Public networks.

## Data and maps

The city catalog is a static OpenStreetMap snapshot, not a live transit feed. Amsterdam, Rotterdam and Utrecht appear together in Explore, and plans may include places from all three cities. A plan's selected city is its starting/reference city. Adding the first place from Explore or Discover automatically saves the tab draft; later additions update the same current plan. **Use this plan** selects a saved plan for map additions, and **Review current plan** opens it. Unsaved draft content and the current plan ID use tab `sessionStorage`. Saved plans live in in-memory H2: they survive browser refresh while Spring is running and reset when the backend process stops.

There may be at most **6 saved plans**, each with **0–12 places**. Plan names allow **30 characters** and descriptions **500**. Creation allocates a numbered name when necessary (`My day out`, `My day out-1`, …). At capacity, existing plans remain editable; creating another returns an explanation to use or delete an existing plan. Autosave retries reuse the same UUID, and a failed save keeps the draft in its tab with a retry action. Saved-plan name, description, removal and ordering edits use **Save changes**.

The MapLibre map loads the public [OpenFreeMap](https://openfreemap.org/) Liberty style and needs an internet connection. It shows the three-city overview; pan and zoom to explore each area. An isolated tile, glyph or sprite failure can leave a partially rendered map without a Retry control. A startup timeout or WebGL loss can show the explicit map fallback and Retry action. Search, place cards and plan workflows remain usable when map resources fail; this is not an offline-map guarantee. MapLibre keeps map attribution visible. See [OpenStreetMap copyright and licensing](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright) and [the photo attribution manifest](frontend/public/assets/catalog/assets-attribution.json) for the featured photos' sources, authors, licenses and change notes.

## Checks

Backend tests and executable archive (from the repository root):

```powershell
Set-Location .\backend
.\gradlew.bat clean test bootJar
```

Frontend checks (from the repository root):

```powershell
Set-Location .\frontend
npm.cmd run lint
npm.cmd run typecheck
npm.cmd run test
npm.cmd run build
```

For browser E2E, start the backend in a separate terminal, then from `frontend/` install Chromium once and run:

```powershell
npx playwright install chromium
npm.cmd run test:e2e
```

The suite starts Angular on port 4300 and uses the real Spring/H2 API on 8080. Its regular map style is deterministic; it does not prove public tile delivery. An optional external style smoke is:

```powershell
$env:HOPON_LIVE_MAP_SMOKE = '1'
npm.cmd run test:e2e -- --grep 'real OpenFreeMap'
Remove-Item Env:\HOPON_LIVE_MAP_SMOKE
```

That smoke checks the style response and map readiness, not rendered tiles.

The [manual phone checklist](docs/HOP-006-manual-qa-checklist.md), [recruitment demo outline](docs/HOP-006-recruitment-demo.md), and [release evidence](docs/HOP-006-release-verification.md) keep automated and user-run checks separate. The [HOP-007 design and validation notes](docs/HOP-007-design-notes.md) describe the current planner behavior and responsive UI checks.

## Troubleshooting

- **Port 8080 is occupied:** identify the listening process with the PowerShell steps in the [manual QA checklist](docs/HOP-006-manual-qa-checklist.md). Do not stop an unknown process or start a second backend. Restarting Spring clears saved H2 plans.
- **Map resources fail:** isolated tile errors may leave gaps without a Retry control. If startup reaches the explicit fallback, check internet access and use **Retry map**. OpenFreeMap is external; catalog and plan workflows remain available.
- **Playwright cannot find Chromium:** run `npx playwright install chromium` from `frontend/`.
- **Phone cannot connect:** confirm the phone and PC share a trusted Wi-Fi and that the Private firewall allows the Angular server. Do not open port 8080 or add router port forwarding.
